---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import CalculatorLab from '../components/CalculatorLab.astro';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Probability Calculator for Numbers and Sequences" description="Calculate event and ordered-sequence probabilities for a uniform 0-9 model. Compare theoretical probabilities with browser-generated trials and inspect the assumptions.">
  <div class="container"><Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Calculator' }]} /></div>
  <header class="page-header container"><p class="eyebrow">The probability desk</p><h1>Count the possibilities.<br /><em>Understand the chance.</em></h1><p>Work from possible outcomes, calculate an exact ordered sequence, and compare a theoretical probability with a local sample.</p></header>
  <div class="container tool-layout"><CalculatorLab /><aside class="tool-sidebar"><span class="eyebrow">Change the question</span><h2>Change the calculation.</h2><dl class="probability-list"><div><dt>One exact digit</dt><dd>1 / 10 = <strong>10%</strong></dd></div><div><dt>Big or Small individually</dt><dd>5 / 10 = <strong>50%</strong></dd></div><div><dt>Violet (0 or 5)</dt><dd>2 / 10 = <strong>20%</strong></dd></div><div><dt>7, then 7</dt><dd>1 / 10 × 1 / 10 = <strong>1%</strong></dd></div></dl><a class="text-link" href="/number-probability/">Read the full explanation <Icon /></a></aside></div>
  <section class="section container tool-explanation"><div class="prose"><h2>What the model assumes</h2><p>There are ten equally likely digits, 0 through 9, and draws are independent. These assumptions describe this educational model. They do not establish the probabilities or fairness of an external service.</p><h2>One draw or several draws?</h2><p>For one draw, count distinct digits that satisfy the question. For a specified sequence across independent draws, multiply each position’s probability. Big, Big, Small has probability 1/2 × 1/2 × 1/2 = 1/8, or 12.5%.</p><p>The sequence answer applies to that next block in that order. It is not the probability of finding the pattern somewhere in a longer history. Learn more in <a href="/patterns-and-randomness/">the streaks and randomness guide</a>.</p><h2>Why color groups need care</h2><p>Red and Violet overlap at 0. “Red or Violet” contains six distinct digits: 0, 2, 4, 5, 6, and 8. Its probability is 60%, rather than 70%. Big and Red contains only 6 and 8, so its probability is 20%, not the 25% obtained by incorrectly assuming independence within a single draw.</p><p>See the <a href="/wingo-rules/#color-classifications">classification table</a> and the <a href="https://openstax.org/books/introductory-statistics-2e/pages/3-3-two-basic-rules-of-probability">OpenStax probability rules</a>.</p><h2>Read the comparison</h2><p>Observed frequency is the number of matching draws divided by the sample size. Expected count is sample size × theoretical probability and can be fractional. The gap is shown in percentage points. A large sample can still differ from the theoretical value; none of these results predicts the next draw.</p><div class="takeaway"><p>Calculations work without a random source. Sampling needs the browser random-value API. No sample is sent to an external service.</p></div></div></section>
</BaseLayout>
